Closes the one thing ADR 0002 said no passive run could prove: that a REAL
interface/address/route change is delivered through the IP Helper
notification callbacks, end to end, and how - including the narrowed
question logs 18/19 left open (does `AF_UNSPEC` deliver a real
notification once, or once per address family, the way it does its
synthetic initial one). Also the first real data on burst behaviour and on
what a notification's Row actually contains.

## Where and how

A separate, physical Windows test machine (Windows 11 Home 26200,
Intel i5-10300H, admin SSH with key auth), not the build machine. Two of its
adapters are safe to change freely:

- `Ethernet` (ifIndex 14, Realtek PCIe GbE): cable unplugged, so
  administrative up/down can be driven without any carrier involved.
- `Ethernet 2` (ifIndex 16, VirtualBox Host-Only, Up, 192.168.56.1/24):
  a virtual adapter with real link state, safe for addresses, routes, and
  admin up/down. No VM was running on it (`VBoxManage list runningvms`
  empty).

The SSH path (`Wi-Fi`, ifIndex 2) and the WSL `vEthernet` (61) were never
touched. Adapter states, addresses and routes were verified identical
before and after each run.

`spike.exe` was built from the working tree of `spikes/windows-ip-helper/`
(which now carries a `-requery` flag: on each real notification, fetch the
full row by its key with `GetIpInterfaceEntry`/`GetIfEntry2Ex`/
`GetUnicastIpAddressEntry` and log the actual state), copied over, and
run twice for a 105 s window each, while a PowerShell driver applied the
same timestamped sequence of real changes both times:

    A1  Disable-NetAdapter Ethernet        (admin DOWN, no carrier)     wait 6 s
    A2  Enable-NetAdapter  Ethernet        (admin UP)                   wait 10 s
    B1  New-NetIPAddress   Ethernet 2  192.168.56.77/24                 wait 6 s
    B2  Remove-NetIPAddress            192.168.56.77                    wait 6 s
    C1  New-NetRoute       10.99.0.0/24 via 192.168.56.2 on Ethernet 2  wait 6 s
    C2  Remove-NetRoute    10.99.0.0/24                                 wait 6 s
    D   burst: 10 addresses (192.168.56.101-110) added back-to-back,
        then removed back-to-back, no sleeps                            wait 10 s
    E1  Disable-NetAdapter Ethernet 2      (admin DOWN of an Up adapter with an address)
    E2  Enable-NetAdapter  Ethernet 2      (admin UP)

Run 1: default registration (`AF_UNSPEC`, one handle per notification
type). Run 2: `-split-family` (AF_INET and AF_INET6 registered separately,
two handles per type). Everything restored in a `finally` block either
way. Raw logs (`spike-default.log`, `spike-split.log`, both timelines)
kept with the results, not committed.

## Headline: real notifications DO arrive, and AF_UNSPEC delivers each one ONCE

    run            NOTIFY lines   initial   REAL
    AF_UNSPEC         109            6       103
    per-family        106            6       100

Per step, with the family label folded for addr/route rows (the only
difference there is `reqFamily=0` vs `2`/`23`, i.e. which registration the
callback came through) and kept for iface rows (where it is the API's own
data model - see below):

| step | change | notification | AF_UNSPEC | per-family |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| A1 | Realtek admin DOWN | `addr/DeleteInstance/if=14` | 2 | 2 |
| | | `iface/DeleteInstance/if=14/fam=2` | 1 | 1 |
| | | `iface/DeleteInstance/if=14/fam=23` | 1 | 1 |
| | | `iface/ParameterChange/if=14/fam=2` | 1 | 1 |
| | | `iface/ParameterChange/if=14/fam=23` | 1 | 1 |
| | | `route/DeleteInstance/if=14` | 5 | 5 |
| A2 | Realtek admin UP | `addr/AddInstance/if=14` | 2 | 2 |
| | | `iface/AddInstance/if=14/fam=2`, `/fam=23` | 1, 1 | 1, 1 |
| | | `iface/ParameterChange/if=14` (both families together) | 3 | 3 |
| | | `route/AddInstance/if=14` | 5 | 5 |
| B1 | add 192.168.56.77/24 | `addr/AddInstance/if=16` | 1 | 1 |
| | | `addr/ParameterChange/if=16` (DAD -> Preferred) | 1 | 1 |
| | | `route/AddInstance/if=16` (the /32 host route) | 1 | 1 |
| B2 | remove it | `addr/DeleteInstance/if=16`, `route/DeleteInstance/if=16` | 1, 1 | 1, 1 |
| C1 | add route | `route/AddInstance/if=16` | 1 | 1 |
| C2 | remove route | `route/DeleteInstance/if=16` | 1 | 1 |
| D | burst 10 add + 10 remove | `addr/AddInstance/if=16` | **10** | **10** |
| | | `addr/DeleteInstance/if=16` | **10** | **10** |
| E1 | host-only admin DOWN | `addr/DeleteInstance/if=16` | 2 | 2 |
| | | `iface/DeleteInstance/if=16/fam=2`, `/fam=23` | 1, 1 | 1, 1 |
| | | `iface/ParameterChange/if=16/fam=2`, `/fam=23` | 1, 1 | 1, 1 |
| | | `route/DeleteInstance/if=16` | 8 | 8 |
| E2 | host-only admin UP | `addr/AddInstance/if=16` | 2 | 2 |
| | | `addr/ParameterChange/if=16` | 2 | 2 |
| | | `iface/AddInstance/if=16/fam=2`, `/fam=23` | 1, 1 | 1, 1 |
| | | `iface/ParameterChange/if=16/fam=2`, `/fam=23` | 2, 2 | 2, 2 |
| | | `route/AddInstance/if=16` | 8 | 8 |

Every substantive count is identical between the two registrations. Where
the per-family run shows 5 route deletes as 2 (v4) + 3 (v6), the
`AF_UNSPEC` run shows 5 through its one handle - not 10. The burst of ten
address adds produced exactly ten `AddInstance` callbacks under both, not
twenty. **So the answer to the question ADR 0002 narrowed to is: no -
`AF_UNSPEC` does not duplicate real notifications. Only its synthetic
initial callback is per-family (log 18). A future adapter can register
once with `AF_UNSPEC` and does not need to de-duplicate.**

The only rows that differ between runs are `iface/ParameterChange` on
ifIndex 2 - the Wi-Fi, which nobody touched - at different steps and in
different numbers (0-4 per step, plus a pair 1.3 s before step A1 and a
pair after the sequence ended). These are spontaneous parameter changes on
a live wireless interface (Windows re-evaluating metrics/state as other
adapters come and go, and on its own). Real-world background noise that
an adapter must expect and must not turn into `link.*` events.

## Interface notifications are per IP family by design - not a duplicate

An adapter admin change yields TWO `iface` callbacks, one with
`family=2` and one with `family=23`, in BOTH runs. That is the data model:
`NotifyIpInterfaceChange` reports `MIB_IPINTERFACE_ROW`s, and one NDIS
interface has one IPv4 and one IPv6 IP-interface row. The recorder's
`ports.InterfaceObservation` is per link, not per family, so the future
adapter keys on `InterfaceLuid` and folds the two rows into one
observation - a design note, not a bug in either side.

## What `-requery` showed: the callback Row is a key, not a snapshot

Every `iface` callback row said `connected=false` - including for the Wi-Fi
that was Up the whole time - and every `addr` row said `dadState=0`.
Exactly what Microsoft documents: the Row passed to a change callback
carries only key fields. `-requery` fetched the real state by that key:

- B1, address add: `AddInstance` at +0.9 s with the re-queried row at
  `dadState=Tentative`; then **3.4 s later** three things within 12 ms:
  `route/AddInstance 192.168.56.77/32` (the host route), and
  `addr/ParameterChange` whose re-query shows `dadState=Preferred`. So an
  address is "there" at AddInstance and "usable" at the ParameterChange
  that follows DAD - an adapter that wants Linux-equivalent
  `l3.addr_added` semantics should record at Preferred, or record both
  states.
- D, the burst: the ten addresses were removed ~0.8 s after being added,
  before DAD completed - so no `ParameterChange` and no `/32` host routes
  for any of them (unlike B1). Consistent with the above; also shows the
  callback queue delivered all 20 events for a change rate of ~12/s with
  nothing dropped or coalesced.
- A1, Realtek disable: the notifications arrive in the order
  `ParameterChange` (both families) -> `DeleteInstance` (v6) ->
  `DeleteInstance` (v4), and **re-querying races the change itself**:
  `GetIpInterfaceEntry` already returned `Element not found` at the
  `ParameterChange`, while `GetIfEntry2Ex` still answered
  (`adminStatus=up operStatus=down mediaConnectState=disconnected`) and
  only at the last `DeleteInstance` showed `adminStatus=down
  mediaConnectState=unknown`. `GetUnicastIpAddressEntry` for the
  interface's link-local v4/v6 addresses was likewise `Element not
  found` by the time the callback ran. An adapter must treat "not found"
  on re-query as "gone", never as an error, and must not assume the
  state it re-queries is the state that caused the callback.

## Latency and ordering

Time from the PowerShell cmdlet starting to the first related callback:

    A1 1.16 s (Disable-NetAdapter's own duration)   A2 0.22 s   B1 0.90 s (New-NetIPAddress's own)
    B2 0.05 s   C1 0.08 s   C2 0.05 s   D 0.04 s   E1 0.15 s   E2 0.11 s

i.e. tens of milliseconds once the change is actually made; the larger
numbers are the cmdlets, not the notification path. Comfortably inside
the one-second resolution the recorder's timeline cares about.

## What this does and does not close (against ADR 0002's list)

- Real change delivered end to end: **CLOSED** - interface, address and
  route, v4 and v6, add/delete/parameter-change, all observed.
- `AF_UNSPEC` real-notification duplication: **CLOSED** - it does not.
- Burst/loss: **CLOSED for this scale** - 20 events in ~1.6 s, none lost.
  Not a stress test; nothing here says what happens at thousands/s.
- Interface-row filter (log 19) against a real transition: the two
  adapters used here (physical Realtek, VirtualBox host-only) are both in
  the filter's "keep" class; the disable/enable transitions produced
  `DeleteInstance`/`AddInstance` on exactly those ifIndexes. Consistent
  with the filter; not a full re-test of it.
- Sleep/resume: **NOT tested** - the machine is reached over Wi-Fi; a
  sleep would drop the SSH connection and a laptop may not wake remotely.
  Needs a local console or a VM with ACPI control.
- VPN connect/disconnect: **NOT tested** - no VPN client or configuration
  exists on that machine, and none should be invented for a test.

## Verification

- `cd spikes/windows-ip-helper && go vet ./... && go build`: clean, from
  the working tree including the `-requery` code.
- Both runs exited normally after their 105 s window; `NOTIFY` counts
  above are `Select-String` counts over the raw logs, the per-step table
  is from bucketing each `NOTIFY` line's timestamp into the driver's
  `STEP ... BEGIN` windows (the driver's timeline file was UTF-16 as
  PowerShell writes it - decoded as such, which is why an earlier pass of
  the same analysis put every event "outside any step").
- Adapters after each run: identical to before (`2:Wi-Fi=Up,
  8:Bluetooth=Disconnected, 14:Ethernet=Disconnected, 16:Ethernet 2=Up,
  61:vEthernet=Up`); IPv4 on `Ethernet 2` back to only `192.168.56.1`;
  route `10.99.0.0/24` absent. Re-verified once more after cleanup.
